  "answer": "Higher-authority sources indicate that education startups are **not** the best ICP, despite the sales material's enthusiasm. While [NL-009#NL-009-S04] calls them a \"phenomenal ICP,\" it also contains a caveat to \"do not over-index on them for large-scale revenue quotas\" and to \"prioritize our core regulated mid-market and enterprise segments.\" \n\nThe official performance metrics in [NL-001#NL-001-S04] state that the ICP \"is concentrated in mid-market and enterprise companies operating in highly regulated fields,\" and that SMBs (which include technology startups) \"show significantly higher churn rates and lower expansion potential\" with churn at 4.8% and NPS at 31.\n\nThe Q2 2026 business review in [NL-015#NL-015-S03] confirms this with data: SMB churn is 4.8% vs 2.1% for Mid-Market and 1.3% for Enterprise, and SMB expansion revenue is only $18,000 vs $64,000 for Mid-Market and $91,000 for Enterprise. The CEO explicitly stated, \"our Ideal Customer Profile (ICP) is regulated mid-market and enterprise organizations,\" concluding the company \"cannot pivot our strategy to chase quick SMB sales that churn out in six months.\" The same source notes the analytics data from NL-012 corroborates this view, confirming the \"validity of the regulated enterprise ICP.\"\n\nANSWER_STATUS: answer\nUSED_MEMORY_CLAIMS: none",
